The two methods in `BeanPostProcessor` run at different points in the bean lifecycle, and the distinction determines which features each hook can implement.

**`postProcessBeforeInitialization(Object bean, String beanName)`**
- Runs after dependency injection but before `@PostConstruct`
- Where Spring processes annotations that configure the bean — `@Autowired`, `@Value`, `@Resource`
- The bean has its dependencies but has not yet executed its initialization logic

**`postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String beanName)`**
- Runs after `@PostConstruct` and all initialization callbacks
- Where Spring creates **proxies** — the method can return a different object than the one it received
- That replacement becomes the bean other components inject
- `@Transactional`, `@Async`, `@Cacheable`, and AOP aspects all create proxies in this hook

```java
@Component
public class TimingBeanPostProcessor implements BeanPostProcessor {
    @Override
    public Object postProcessBeforeInitialization(Object bean, String beanName) {
        return bean;
    }

    @Override
    public Object postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String beanName) {
        if (bean.getClass().isAnnotationPresent(Timed.class)) {
            return createTimingProxy(bean);
        }
        return bean;
    }
}
```

**Why returning a different object matters**
- The original `OrderService` instance is created, initialized, then replaced by a proxy that adds transactional behavior around each method call
- Every other bean that injects `OrderService` gets the proxy, not the original
- This is the fundamental mechanism behind every annotation-driven aspect in Spring

The most practical way to understand `BeanPostProcessor` is to build one. The example creates a custom `@Timed` annotation that automatically logs how long each method takes — similar to Micrometer or Spring AOP, but implemented from scratch to show the mechanism.

The processor scans every bean for methods annotated with `@Timed`. When it finds one, it wraps the original bean in a CGLIB proxy that intercepts method calls and measures execution time. This is the same pattern Spring uses internally for `@Transactional` and `@Cacheable`.

```java
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
public @interface Timed {}

@Component
public class TimingPostProcessor implements BeanPostProcessor {
    @Override
    public Object postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String name) {
        boolean hasTimed = Arrays.stream(bean.getClass().getMethods())
            .anyMatch(m -> m.isAnnotationPresent(Timed.class));
        if (!hasTimed) return bean;

        return Enhancer.create(bean.getClass(), (MethodInterceptor) (obj, method, args, proxy) -> {
            if (!method.isAnnotationPresent(Timed.class))
                return proxy.invokeSuper(obj, args);
            long start = System.nanoTime();
            Object result = proxy.invokeSuper(obj, args);
            log.info("{}.{} took {}ms", name, method.getName(),
                (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000);
            return result;
        });
    }
}
```

The key insight is the **return value**. The `postProcessAfterInitialization` method returns either the original bean (no processing needed) or a new object (the proxy). Spring replaces the bean in the container with whatever this method returns. This is why `@Transactional` works — the bean registered in the container is a proxy, not the original class.

**Practical performance note**
- Proxy creation happens once at startup for singleton beans — no per-call cost
- CGLIB proxies add roughly 1-2 microseconds per method call — negligible for most applications
- For post-processors that wrap thousands of beans, the startup time impact can be measurable; profile before optimizing

Running both container types side-by-side makes the differences concrete. `BeanFactory` does lazy, on-demand instantiation; `ApplicationContext` eagerly pre-instantiates singletons and publishes lifecycle events.

```java
public class ContainerComparison {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("--- BeanFactory (lazy) ---");
        DefaultListableBeanFactory bf = new DefaultListableBeanFactory();
        new XmlBeanDefinitionReader(bf).loadBeanDefinitions(
            new ClassPathResource("beans.xml"));
        // Singletons NOT yet instantiated — constructors haven't run.
        UserService svc1 = bf.getBean(UserService.class);   // constructor runs HERE
        UserService svc2 = bf.getBean(UserService.class);   // same instance cached

        System.out.println("--- ApplicationContext (eager + events) ---");
        ApplicationContext ctx = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(AppConfig.class);
        // All singletons already constructed, @PostConstruct already executed,
        // ContextRefreshedEvent already published BEFORE this line.
        UserService svc3 = ctx.getBean(UserService.class);
    }
}

@Configuration
@ComponentScan("com.example")
class AppConfig {}

@Component
class UserService {
    @PostConstruct
    void init() { System.out.println("UserService initialized"); }
    @EventListener
    public void onReady(ContextRefreshedEvent e) { /* only ApplicationContext fires this */ }
}
```

- With `BeanFactory`, the constructor of `UserService` does not run until `getBean()` is called.
- With `ApplicationContext`, all singleton constructors, `@PostConstruct` callbacks, and `BeanPostProcessor` hooks run during `new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(...)` — fail-fast on misconfiguration.
- `@EventListener` only works with `ApplicationContext`, which is why Spring Boot always uses it.
- Use `BeanFactory` only for memory-constrained tools where startup time beats safety; use `ApplicationContext` for every real application.

Writing a custom `Condition` is straightforward — implement the interface, check whatever you need from the `ConditionContext`, and return a boolean.

**What `ConditionContext` exposes**
- `Environment` — properties from any source
- `BeanDefinitionRegistry` — existing bean definitions (not live beans)
- `ClassLoader` — for classpath checks
- `ResourceLoader` — for file existence checks

**Practical use case: feature flags**
- Instead of cluttering code with `if (featureEnabled)` checks, conditionally register the entire bean graph for a feature based on a property
- The condition evaluates at bean definition time — before any bean is instantiated — so disabled features have **zero runtime cost**

```java
public class FeatureEnabledCondition implements Condition {
    @Override
    public boolean matches(ConditionContext context,
                           AnnotatedTypeMetadata metadata) {
        Map<String, Object> attrs = metadata
            .getAnnotationAttributes(ConditionalOnFeature.class.getName());
        String feature = (String) attrs.get("value");
        return "true".equals(
            context.getEnvironment().getProperty("feature." + feature + ".enabled"));
    }
}

@Target({ElementType.TYPE, ElementType.METHOD})
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Conditional(FeatureEnabledCondition.class)
public @interface ConditionalOnFeature {
    String value();
}

@Configuration
@ConditionalOnFeature("dark-mode")
public class DarkModeConfig {
    @Bean public ThemeService themeService() { return new DarkThemeService(); }
}
```

**What happens when feature is disabled**
- When `feature.dark-mode.enabled` is absent or `false`, the entire `DarkModeConfig` class is skipped
- No `ThemeService` bean exists in the context
- Cleaner than wrapping business logic in conditionals — the bean graph itself reflects active features

**Spring Boot's built-in condition mechanisms**
- `@ConditionalOnClass` checks the classloader
- `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` checks the registry
- `@ConditionalOnProperty` checks the environment
